#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "4088c5c5-6f69-54f3-9029-3ba52f5b00d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9435" :question/problem "By setting $y = t$ where $t$ is a parameter, find a definite integral with respect to $t$ that is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\, \\textrm ds$, where $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x = y^2$ traversed between the points $(1,1)$ and $(9, 3)$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3689/Source/Images/a7065ee8203134b582bc36dc9392dd61.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y)$. Therefore, by setting $y = t$, we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain\n\n$$\n1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nis defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= 2ti + j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2t)^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{t^{2}}{t} \\\\\n&= t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{x}{y}ds &= ∫_{1}^{3}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4t^{2} + 1}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "f025deb6-7c71-5d15-9966-52f7d5eeab80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9463" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} y^3e^{-x} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\ln{y}$ from $(0, 1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac32\\ln 2, 2\\sqrt{2}\\right)$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3689/Source/Images/8e10ad385bc31844a4b0f2bf68c467e8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y)$. Therefore, by setting $y=t$, we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain\n\n$$\n1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nis defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\ln t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t}i + j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{t})^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{t^{2}} + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2} + 1}{t^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{\\mid t \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = y^3e^{-x}\n$$\n\nthen we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= t^{3}e^{-\\ln t} \\\\\n&= t^{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\\n&= t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)y^{3}e^{-x}ds &= ∫_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t^{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu = t^2 + 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}dt &= ∫_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u^{3}} \\mid _{2}^{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{9^{3}} - \\sqrt{2^{3}}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{27 - 2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ac86d18-0608-5583-b9c7-c7f6edf37c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177365} {:answer/id #uuid "e0dcd06e-fb31-5a97-8d6c-6bc74d4db44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177366} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7279f3-67ed-5139-aebe-5d7d64a44fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{47}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177367} {:answer/id #uuid "3b337070-2da3-5a92-a635-740256268107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177368} {:answer/id #uuid "3efdd6d2-cd5f-5e8a-ad19-19f5db0751fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b337070-2da3-5a92-a635-740256268107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177368} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c9c876-c3a8-5b4d-84ca-cbb8b8293da8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177364}] :question/id #uuid "9a72d2a3-84b0-5b95-afa8-d5133b8bf8f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122271" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n\n$∫_(C)\\frac{24x}{y}ds$,\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} = 8x$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8}, 3)$."}]}
